[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Character Animation in Unity: Animator Controllers, Blend Trees, and Avatar Masks"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creating fluid and responsive character movement is a cornerstone of modern game development. In Unity, the animation system relies on three powerful components working together: Animator Controllers to manage state flow, Blend Trees to smoothly transition between motion variations, and Avatar Masks to isolate animations to specific body parts."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Animator Controllers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Animator Controller acts as the brain for your animated characters. It uses a state machine to determine which animation should play based on parameters (Float, Int, Bool, and Trigger) passed from your C# scripts."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"States: Represent individual animations or Blend Trees."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transitions: Define conditions and blending durations for moving between states."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parameters: Variables managed by code that drive transition conditions."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Setting up Blend Trees for Fluid Movement"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of creating a web of individual transitions for walking, running, and idle, a 1D or 2D Blend Tree blends multiple animations together based on one or two parameters. For example, a 1D Blend Tree can blend from Idle (speed = 0) to Walk (speed = 1) to Run (speed = 2)."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerMovement : MonoBehaviour {
    private Animator animator;
    private float currentSpeed;

    void Start() {
        animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
    }

    void Update() {
        currentSpeed = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");
        animator.SetFloat("Speed", currentSpeed);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Utilizing Avatar Masks for Layered Animation"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Avatar Masks allow you to apply animations to specific parts of a humanoid avatar. This is essential for upper-body actions (like reloading a weapon or swinging a sword) while the lower body continues to run or walk."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Right-click in your Project window -> Create -> Avatar Mask."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open the mask inspector and toggle off the body parts you wish to exclude (e.g., legs and feet for an upper-body attack layer)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In your Animator Controller, create a new Layer, assign your Avatar Mask to it, and set its Weight to 1."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining Animator Controllers, Blend Trees, and Avatar Masks, you can build a modular, high-performance animation system that responds dynamically to player input while keeping your state machine clean and maintainable."}]
